# Copy to /etc/sponsor/sponsor.env on the box, chmod 0600, owned by the sponsor user.
# systemd reads this as an EnvironmentFile: KEY=value, no export, no shell quoting.
#
# ⚠️ **This file is SHARED by every network and by the router.** What belongs here is what is the same
# whichever chain a wallet is on: the token, the origin, and the sponsorship policy. Everything that
# differs per network — MIDNIGHT_NETWORK, the port, the DUST snapshot, the backlog — lives in
# `sponsor.<network>.env`, which the unit reads *after* this one and can therefore override.
#
# The seed is deliberately NOT here. CHAIN_SEED_FILE points at it instead, because an env var is
# visible in `ps` and in anything that dumps the environment on a crash.

# Shared secret with the Worker, and with the router in front of the wallets. Generate with
# `openssl rand -hex 32` and set the same value via `bunx wrangler secret put SPONSOR_TOKEN`. A tunnel is
# not authentication, and neither is being on loopback — anything else on the box could otherwise reach
# the wallets.
SPONSOR_TOKEN=

# Where settlement reports go. The Worker owns every row the app reads; this host only reports outcomes.
CONTENT_ORIGIN=https://pnyx.pxkq.uk

# What this sponsor will pay for. Both required, and both policy rather than plumbing: without them the
# sponsor would balance any proven transaction anyone sends, including one against an unrelated contract
# (docs/ABUSE_MITIGATION.md §1).
#
# ⚠️ **The same on every network, and that is not a shortcut.** A code digest is a fact about the
# compiled template, not about a deployment — one contract per identity and one per circle, each at an
# address derived from arguments this host never sees. So the digest recognises any of them on any chain,
# and it changes on exactly the event that should invalidate it: a recompile.
#
#   curl -s https://artifacts.pxkq.uk/identity/manifest.json | jq -r .codeDigest
#   curl -s https://pnyx.pxkq.uk/chain/artifacts/circle | jq -r .codeDigest
IDENTITY_CODE_DIGEST=
CIRCLE_CODE_DIGEST=

# The permit contract and the name template. Optional: a deployment that has not reached handles yet is
# a real composition, and refusing to boot over it would be inventing a dependency. Absent means those
# calls are refused, which is what "no handles here" means.
# PAYMENT_CODE_DIGEST=
# HANDLE_CODE_DIGEST=

# 0600, owned by the sponsor user. A BIP-39 mnemonic — the only format the app can also restore, so the
# wallet paying the fees is inspectable from the product. A hex master seed is still accepted; anything
# else refuses the boot rather than syncing a wallet nobody meant to use.
#
# ⚠️ **One seed, three networks, by design.** The same mnemonic derives a different address per network
# (the HRP differs, the payload does not), so each wallet is a distinct wallet on a distinct chain with
# its own DUST. Shared here so there is one secret on the box rather than three.
CHAIN_SEED_FILE=/etc/sponsor/sponsor-seed

# Proving key material for the four wallet circuits, mirrored by deploy/fetch-prover-keys.sh.
# Not optional in practice: balancing a device-proven transaction adds unproven zswap/dust spends, so
# something has to prove them, and this host runs no proof server (docs/SPONSOR_HOST.md). Contract-
# independent and chain-independent, so one directory serves every network.
CHAIN_PROVER_KEYS_DIR=/var/lib/sponsor/prover-keys
# PROOF_SERVER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:6300

# The routing table the router serves, and where an un-networked call goes. Read by
# sponsor-router.service; the wallets ignore it.
#
# ⚠️ **The default exists so a Worker deployed before multi-network keeps working.** Its body carried no
# network at all, and the router logs every call it has to default. Remove it once nothing sends an
# un-networked submission.
SPONSOR_NETWORKS=preview:3401,preprod:3402,mainnet:3403
SPONSOR_DEFAULT_NETWORK=preprod

# CHAIN_MAX_RETRIES=

# ── Invite minting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What lets this host issue a circle invite on demand: it builds and proves an `issueInvite` contract
# call itself, which is the one job on this box that reads chain state before writing to it.
#
# ⚠️ INVITE_AUTHORITY_ROOT is the second secret this host holds, after the wallet seed. Every gated
# circle's invite authority derives from it (`invite-authority.ts`), so rotating it **orphans every
# circle that already delegated** — their contracts still commit to the old derivation and every mint
# refuses as "not this circle's invite authority" until each founder re-delegates. Generate once:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# INVITE_AUTHORITY_ROOT=

# The Rust transaction builder and `issueInvite`'s ZK artifacts, both put there by
# `deploy/push-invite-minter.sh`. ⚠️ Re-run that script after every recompile of `circle.compact`:
# the artifacts must be the compile the circles were deployed from, and the service refuses to mint
# when the mirrored digest and CIRCLE_CODE_DIGEST disagree.
# MIDNIGHT_TXGEN_BIN=/var/lib/sponsor/bin/pnyx_txgen
# CIRCLE_ARTIFACTS_DIR=/var/lib/sponsor/circle-artifacts

# Where the KZG SRS lives. Defaults to $CHAIN_PROVER_KEYS_DIR/params, which is where
# `deploy/fetch-prover-keys.sh` already mirrors it — set only if the two are separated.
# MIDNIGHT_PARAMS_DIR=

# ── proposal.compact ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One contract per proposal, and creating one is a *deploy*. Its own digest, never the circle's: the
# 2026-08-25 split moved voting into a separate contract, and until this existed both a proposal
# deploy and a vote were refused with nothing naming the cause.
# PROPOSAL_CODE_DIGEST=
